# Model Behavior Calibration

This rule corrects agent defaults that do not match what this repository needs. It applies to every
task, not to a specific area of the codebase.

Each section states the default behavior first, then the correction. These are calibration
instructions, not permission to skip anything in `absolute-rules`, `surfaces`, or the
Non-Negotiable Boundaries section of `AGENTS.md`.

## Response length

Default: responses are longer than the task requires, and reducing reasoning depth does not shorten
them. Prompting is the only lever that works.

Lead with the outcome. The first sentence after finishing answers what happened or what was found —
the thing the reader would ask for if they said "just give me the summary". Supporting detail and
reasoning come after, for readers who want them.

Keep output short by being selective about what to include, not by compressing the writing.
Readability matters more than brevity: drop details that do not change what the reader would do
next, and write what remains in complete sentences with technical terms spelled out. Do not
compress into fragments, abbreviations, arrow chains such as `A → B → fails`, or invented shorthand
the reader has no way to decode.

Match the response to the question. A simple question gets a direct answer in prose, not headers and
sections. Use tables only for short enumerable facts, and keep explanation in the surrounding prose
rather than inside cells.

## Written deliverables

Default: files written to disk — notes, memos, ADRs, plans, reports — run longer than their
substance warrants.

Match the length of a written deliverable to what the task needs. Cover the substance, and do not
pad with filler sections, redundant summaries, restated context, or boilerplate headings kept only
because a template listed them. A template section with nothing to record is omitted, not filled
with prose.

This applies to every knowledge directory in `repository-knowledge-tree`. For the contents and
discipline of an implementation note specifically, see `implementation-notes`.

## Narration between tool calls

Default: more narration than a reader needs, including announcements of routine actions.

Before the first tool call, state in one sentence what is about to happen. While working, write
only when there is something to report: a finding that changes the approach, a change of direction,
or a blocker. One sentence each.

Do not narrate routine actions — no "Now I'll read the controller", "Let me check the tests", or
"Looking at the adapter". When the work is done, give the outcome in one or two sentences. Do not
recap every file read and every command run; the reader has been following along.

## Corrections

Default: earlier mistakes are flagged and explained at length, which reads as thrash.

Correct an earlier statement only when the error would change the reader's code, conclusions, or
decisions. State the correction plainly, then continue the task. Combine multiple corrections rather
than enumerating them one by one. For a slip that changes nothing for the reader, make the
correction and move on without noting it.

Do not add apologies or preambles, do not be self-critical, and do not tally past errors. A
follow-up question about earlier work is not evidence that the work was wrong — answer what was
asked. A statement that was accurate needs no correction and no re-audit of how it was phrased or
verified.

## Verification belongs in the main loop

Default: work is already self-checked without being asked. Adding explicit instructions to verify,
re-check, or double-check causes redundant work rather than better work.

Do not add "double-check your answer", "re-verify before responding", or "include a final
verification step" instructions to prompts, skills, or harnesses in this repository. Do not add a
separate verifier pass over work already completed in the main loop, and do not delegate
verification to a subagent.

This concerns *self-review scaffolding only*. It does not reduce any testing obligation: run the
narrowest relevant checks per `AGENTS.md`, and cover success, failure, authorization, and boundary
cases per `testing`. Report results faithfully — if tests fail, say so with the output; if a check
was skipped, say that.

For the narrow case where an adversarial fresh-context review is genuinely warranted — irreversible
blast radius, security boundaries, data migrations — see the `doubt-driven-development` skill, which
defines that threshold.

## Scope

Default: task scope expands, and judgment about what the task should be gets applied without saying
so.

Deliver what was asked, at the scope intended. Interpret ambiguity the way a careful colleague
would: make routine judgment calls without asking, and check in only when different readings would
lead to materially different work.

If the ask appears mistaken or a better approach exists, say so in a sentence and continue with the
task as asked. Do not quietly narrow, widen, or transform it. Do not add features, refactors, or
abstractions the task does not require; a bug fix does not need surrounding cleanup.

Finish the whole task, not the easy part of it, and report completion only when it is fully done. If
part of the work is genuinely blocked, complete everything else and state plainly what is missing
and why. Stop short of actions clearly beyond what the request implies.

## Subagent budget

Default: delegation happens more readily than the work justifies.

Every subagent re-establishes context, re-explores the codebase, and reports back, after which its
report has to be read. Delegate only when the payoff clearly exceeds that overhead.

Delegate for genuinely independent, sizeable tracks — a wide multi-file investigation across
unrelated areas, for example.

Do not delegate:

- work finishable directly in a handful of tool calls, such as a few file reads, a small set of
  edits, or a single search
- review or verification of work already done, per the verification section above

Keep spawn counts low. If one subagent can do the job, use one. Do not split a single modest task
across several. When delegating, brief the subagent precisely the first time rather than launching,
waiting, and re-briefing, and do not re-derive its findings once it reports back. Launch
independent subagents in a single message so they run concurrently.
